Automatically records when the pipeline rejected the row.

2. Data Profiling

Before cleaning data, we inspected the condition of the Raw Layer first.

This is important because:

Profile first. Transform second.

For orders, we ran:

SELECT
    COUNT(*) AS total_rows,
    COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) AS distinct_order_ids,
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE order_id IS NULL) AS missing_order_id,
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE customer_id IS NULL) AS missing_customer_id,
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE total_amount IS NULL) AS missing_amount,
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE total_amount < 0) AS negative_amount,
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE order_date > CURRENT_DATE) AS future_order_date
FROM raw.orders;
Explanation
COUNT(*) AS total_rows

Counts all records.

Our result:

10
COUNT(DISTINCT order_id)

Counts unique orders.

Our result:

5

This immediately showed us something suspicious.

10 rows
5 unique orders

Meaning records were duplicated.

COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE order_id IS NULL)

Counts orders with missing order_id.

COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE customer_id IS NULL)

Checks missing customers.

COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE total_amount IS NULL)

Checks orders with no amount.

COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE total_amount < 0)

Checks impossible/suspicious negative order totals.

COUNT(*) FILTER (
    WHERE order_date > CURRENT_DATE
)

Checks orders dated in the future.

