🧠 Concepts I Learned on Day 2

Data Profiling — inspect the condition of incoming data before transforming it.

Validation — decide whether values are acceptable.

Standardization — convert inconsistent formatting into one format using things like LOWER() and TRIM().

Deduplication — remove duplicate business records while preserving one canonical copy.

Quarantine / Reject Handling — preserve bad records instead of deleting them.

CTE (WITH) — create temporary query results that make complex SQL easier to understand.

ROW_NUMBER() — rank records and identify duplicates.

Window Functions — calculate values across related rows without collapsing them like normal GROUP BY.

Referential Integrity — make sure child records belong to valid parent records.

EXISTS — efficiently check whether a related record exists.

Cross-table Reconciliation — make sure multiple datasets agree with each other.

Transactions (BEGIN / COMMIT) — treat multiple database operations as one controlled unit.

💡 Biggest Day 2 Realization

Before this project, “cleaning data” sounded like:

Remove NULL
Fix spelling
Remove duplicates

But now I understand that real Data Engineering is more structured.

We need to ask:

Is the data complete?
Is it valid?
Is it consistent?
Does it make business sense?
Does it agree with related datasets?
Can we trace rejected records?
Can we safely rerun the pipeline?

The biggest real-world issue we discovered was something we didn't intentionally create:

raw.orders = 10 rows
unique orders = 5

The Orders ingestion had been executed twice.

Instead of simply deleting the duplicates, the Staging Layer identified them, kept one valid copy, and quarantined the additional copies.

That made the lesson much more realistic because it demonstrated why idempotency, deduplication, lineage, and reject handling matter in production pipelines.

